WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:06.480
Thanks to its gruesome imagery and terrifying
tone, 1997's Event Horizon has attained cult

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.000
status in the years since its release.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.070
But the behind-the-scenes story is just as
fascinating as the film itself.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.390
So buckle up and let's take a look back at
the untold truth of Event Horizon.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.330
In the mid-'90s, Paul W.S. Anderson was still
a relatively green director.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:29.579
His debut feature, the 1994 action film Shopping,
was notable mostly for featuring a young Jude

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:31.960
Law in his first leading role.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.680
Unfortunately, the British flick hardly registered
a blip with stateside audiences.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.520
But Anderson's second film, the 1995 video
game adaptation Mortal Kombat, proved to Tinseltown

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.650
brass that he could make a profitable genre
film on a tight budget.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.310
Because of this, he was given a choice of
projects for his next feature.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.630
One of those projects was X-Men, which would
eventually kick off the modern superhero genre

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.500
under the direction of Bryan Singer.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.399
While Anderson was offered the director's
chair on X-Men, he turned it down in favor

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:02.670
of making the sci-fi horror flick.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:07.530
He does admit that it, quote, "probably wasn't
a good career choice," but he just didn't

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.860
want to make another PG-13 film.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.480
On the DVD commentary for Event Horizon, Anderson
said...

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.310
"I definitely had something dark and scary
in my psyche to get out."

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.330
So he gravitated toward Event Horizon and
passed on the opportunity to introduce the

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.500
world to the cinematic version of Wolverine
and all his mutant buddies.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.030
“This is the stupidest thing I've ever heard.”

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:32.880
Event Horizon's original screenplay needed
a bit of surgery before going in front of

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.040
the cameras.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:39.400
This is because the initial version of the
script was a little too close to 1979's Alien.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.979
Similar to the central plot in Ridley Scott's
legendary film, the crew of Anderson's spaceship

00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:49.520
was supposed to encounter something unexpected
on their voyage — alien monsters.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.549
In the first version of the screenplay, a
bunch of evil extraterrestrials had slaughtered

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:56.340
the vessel's crew and were hungry for more.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.810
However, Anderson had something a bit more
unique in mind.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.759
He wanted to tell a ghost story more along
the lines of The Shining, in which the spaceship,

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:10.599
called the Event Horizon, gains a wicked form
of sentience and becomes a crucial character

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:11.599
in the film.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:12.730
"What are you telling me?

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.779
That this ship is alive?"

00:02:14.779 --> 00:02:19.209
Anderson was given free rein by the studio
to reshape the story as he saw fit, as well

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:22.859
as a budget of 60 million dollars to bring
his vision to life.

00:02:22.859 --> 00:02:26.980
The director basically took a scalpel to the
original script, jettisoning the elements

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.590
that would've made it little more than a slightly
spookier Alien rip-off in favor of an altogether

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:33.830
more disturbing project.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:40.400
In reworking Event Horizon to his liking,
Anderson drew heavily on Solaris, a 1972 feature

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.559
from the Soviet Union based on the 1961 novel
by Polish author Stanislaw Lem.

00:02:46.559 --> 00:02:51.249
Solaris is a meditative, dreamy movie, but
it's easy to see the influence of its plot

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:53.420
all over Event Horizon.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:58.370
In Solaris, a psychologist is sent to examine
the crew of a space station that's been orbiting

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.420
a planet for decades.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.329
He finds a borderline hostile, uncooperative
crew inhabiting a station that's been dangerously

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:09.849
neglected, and he soon comes to find that
his friend, a scientist who accompanied the

00:03:09.849 --> 00:03:11.510
original crew, has killed himself.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.469
Then, he begins to see people roaming the
halls of the station who aren't supposed to

00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:18.019
be there — including his late wife.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:22.310
More than one critic, including the great
Roger Ebert, recognized the influence of Solaris

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.639
on Event Horizon upon its release, and Anderson
himself has acknowledged as much.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:30.059
In a 2014 interview with Grantland, the director
said...

00:03:30.059 --> 00:03:35.459
"I love the movies like Solaris, the original
Solaris — the script [for Event Horizon]

00:03:35.459 --> 00:03:37.529
clearly draws from [that film].

00:03:37.529 --> 00:03:42.030
Those kind of meditative European films that
are unsettling but don't really play to a

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:43.529
modern audience.

00:03:43.529 --> 00:03:46.709
By adding that kind of visceral thrill, that's
making it my own."

00:03:47.879 --> 00:03:52.959
Anderson's Solaris-influenced tweak to Philip
Eisner's original screenplay was diabolically

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:53.959
simple.

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:59.010
The Event Horizon, designed to travel at faster-than-light
speeds by breaching the space-time continuum,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.599
had traveled somewhere that it was never meant
to go, and in doing so, it had become infected

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:05.359
with something evil.

00:04:05.359 --> 00:04:09.779
While it's never made crystal clear, the implication
is that the ship has literally been to Hell

00:04:09.779 --> 00:04:11.139
and back.

00:04:11.139 --> 00:04:15.909
And since that basically made the ship itself
the villain of the film, it needed an appropriately

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:17.190
ominous design.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.190
Talking with The Ringer, Anderson recalled
that for the design of the ship, he drew from

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:24.920
Gothic architecture and one iconic site in
particular.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:25.970
The director said...

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:31.200
"Using an architectural cam program, we basically
built Notre Dame Cathedral in the computer,

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.020
and then we pulled it apart, and we used different
elements of it to build the Event Horizon.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.450
So, the towers from Notre Dame became the
engine thruster pods."

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.510
Thanks to the film's beefy budget, the entire
interior of the Event Horizon was constructed

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:49.010
on a cavernous sound stage, and once it was
finished, Anderson knew he was on the right

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.010
track.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:54.320
"[Cast members] would go, 'I love working
with you, Paul, but I hate coming to work.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.320
It was just horrible.'

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.790
[...] It was actually the sets themselves
because the crew couldn't have been nicer.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.390
But there was a disturbing vibe.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:06.950
I really felt like we created a very physical
haunted house for the actors to interact with."

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.000
However, in the end, the ominous sets were
the perfect match for the sinister characters

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.370
that inhabited them.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.960
"I am home."

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:20.380
Anderson would need a veteran of genre films
to effectively shoot his bizarre sets, and

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.690
he got one in Adrian Biddle, who worked on
such instantly iconic films as Aliens and

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:27.290
The Princess Bride.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:31.261
Biddle immediately understood what Anderson
was going for, and with the help of the director

00:05:31.261 --> 00:05:35.540
and production designer Joseph Bennett, he
carefully selected a lighting scheme that

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:41.550
would make the Event Horizon ship appear appropriately
futuristic and sterile in cold artificial

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:46.670
light and take on a whole new personality
when bathed in dim light and darkness.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:51.590
Anderson recalled his co-workers’ contributions
in a 2020 chat with American Cinematographer

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.590
magazine.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:56.270
He said that they spent a lot of time coming
up with a design concept, which they called

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:57.880
"techno-medieval."

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:58.880
He went on to say...

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.480
"When the lights are on, everything looks
very technological and very spaceship-like.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.420
But when the lights go off and the haunting
begins, you start looking at the shapes, and

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.370
the architecture is actually very medieval.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:15.100
We extended that techno-medieval design idea
into as many aspects of the picture's look

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.250
as possible."

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.860
Biddle opted to use colored gels to augment
the film's lighting, a time-honored technique

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.100
that had fallen out of favor, to further heighten
the film's sense of unease.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.210
He explained that he used some sepia brown
coming up from the floor to make viewers uncomfortable

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:32.540
on the ship, as well as flashes of red and
green.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:33.970
He also said...

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:37.801
"Cinematographers generally shy away from
green, because it's not very pleasant, but

00:06:37.801 --> 00:06:42.800
on Event Horizon I used gels to produce that
nasty, horrible green you get from fluorescents.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.150
[...] I was going for that kind of an effect,
to convey the idea that something not very

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:48.750
good is lurking in the ship."

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:54.560
At the time Event Horizon was in production,
Paramount was waiting on another film that

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.800
was running just a bit over schedule, not
to mention over budget.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.030
It might seem counterintuitive today, but
that film — James Cameron's Titanic, which

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.530
would become the highest-grossing movie of
all time — was in no way a sure thing.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:11.900
In fact, Paramount feared that it might have
a disaster on its hands befitting the flick's

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.130
namesake vessel.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:18.580
As such, the studio was hungry for a hit film
it could squeeze out in advance of Titanic's

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:19.810
debut.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.070
According to The Ringer, it settled on Event
Horizon, which presented a slight problem

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:26.020
for Anderson and his crew.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:30.020
The studio shifted production on the film
into overdrive, and as a result, Anderson

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.880
was essentially made to shoot, edit, and oversee
visual effects on the film on an insanely

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.570
tight schedule.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.760
Rather than the usual ten weeks allotted for
editing, he only ended up with four, forcing

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.610
him to work nonstop for that entire period.

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:48.190
And that's evident in some of Event Horizon's
choppy editing work and rough line readings,

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.889
almost certainly the result of Anderson simply
not having time to get as many takes of certain

00:07:52.889 --> 00:07:54.460
scenes as he would have liked.

00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:59.330
Against all odds, Anderson was able to slap
together a cut of the film that was screened

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.250
for test audiences and Paramount executives.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:06.370
Those execs had seen dailies that had highlighted
the flick's sci-fi elements instead of its

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.770
disturbing vibe and graphic violence, but
when they got a load of what Anderson had

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:15.760
turned in, they — along with the test audiences
— had a reaction the director hadn't quite

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:17.350
expected.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:20.240
They were, in a word, disgusted.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.000
Anderson remembered the first, disastrous
test screening on the DVD commentary…

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.240
"You can't underestimate the kind of shock
that this movie had when it was shown to people.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:33.019
[...] People were really, really upset by
it, even by the level of unpleasantness there

00:08:33.019 --> 00:08:34.769
is in [the final cut]."

00:08:34.769 --> 00:08:38.189
And as Anderson explained, the studio felt
the same way as test audiences.

00:08:38.189 --> 00:08:43.419
Executives were, quote, "freaked out" by the
film's grisly, gruesome nature.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:44.550
As the director put it...

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:48.300
"Paramount is the studio [...] known for the
Star Trek franchise, and I don't think that

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:51.819
this was quite the space movie that they had
anticipated."

00:08:51.819 --> 00:08:54.670
Just how gruesome was the first cut of the
film?

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:59.009
We may never know for sure because, so far,
an extended version of Event Horizon has yet

00:08:59.009 --> 00:09:00.589
to materialize.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:05.670
After its initial, disastrous test screening,
though, Anderson cut over 30 minutes of footage.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.449
While these cuts did effectively excise some
of the more graphically unsettling moments

00:09:09.449 --> 00:09:14.809
objected to by the executives, they also had
the unintended effect of giving short shrift

00:09:14.809 --> 00:09:18.730
to the development of the movie's supporting
characters, whose fears and anxieties were

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:22.390
explored in much more unsettling detail than
in the final film.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:28.189
While it's certainly possible that she may
have been overstating matters slightly, Joely

00:09:28.189 --> 00:09:31.810
Richardson, who portrayed Lieutenant Stark,
was creeped out enough while working on the

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:36.990
film that she's said in recent years that
she believed the production to be "cursed."

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:41.829
In a chat with Den of Geek, Richardson described
how one scene involving her and Sam Neill

00:09:41.829 --> 00:09:46.019
required multiple takes, all of which ended
in the same bizarre fashion.

00:09:46.019 --> 00:09:50.390
During rehearsal, they were meant to be at
a console, then there would be a fake explosion,

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.089
and the actors would throw themselves off
their chairs.

00:09:53.089 --> 00:09:54.300
Richardson went on to say...

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:58.240
"When Sam and I did the scene for real, there
was the count of three, and then neither of

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.399
us remember what happened next.

00:10:00.399 --> 00:10:04.329
The explosion went off, and we woke up a few
moments later on the floor.

00:10:04.329 --> 00:10:06.480
That happened every single time!"

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.339
During another scene, she and the ship's captain,
played by Laurence Fishburne, were required

00:10:10.339 --> 00:10:14.490
to crawl through a doorway just before a heavy
steel door slams shut.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.930
Richardson said that due to his vision being
obscured by smoke, a special effect technician

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:23.220
closed the door just a bit too soon — causing
her foot to get stuck in the door.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:24.220
The actress said...

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:25.389
"It was jinxed!

00:10:25.389 --> 00:10:27.640
But we all managed to have a laugh regardless."

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:33.630
Anderson's pared-down cut of Event Horizon
was released in August 1997 to an absolute

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:35.019
critical drubbing.

00:10:35.019 --> 00:10:40.399
Even today, after having undergone a significant
critical reappraisal, it holds a dismal 28%

00:10:40.399 --> 00:10:42.190
rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:46.430
Audiences were also turned off, and rather
than the late summer hit that Paramount was

00:10:46.430 --> 00:10:51.560
expecting, the flick turned out to be a massive
flop, grossing a mere $26 million — not

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:53.110
even half of its budget.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:57.439
Fortunately for Paramount, the film that the
studio had feared was headed for disaster,

00:10:57.439 --> 00:10:59.230
Titanic, saved the day.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:02.300
"I'm the king of the world."

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:06.360
Of course, most cult classics get this kind
of initial reception.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.670
And while Event Horizon's flaws are many,
it can hardly be denied that it's a film that

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.279
sticks in the memory of viewers.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:17.269
In particular, Neill's unhinged performance
is a thing of wonder, and the design of the

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:22.440
doomed ship's interiors, complemented by Adrian
Biddle's expert camera work, is a remarkable

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:23.440
achievement.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:28.089
Still, one can't help but wonder what the
film might have been had Paramount not forced

00:11:28.089 --> 00:11:32.889
Anderson to work on an impossible schedule
or if that hellish test screening hadn't gone

00:11:32.889 --> 00:11:33.939
so poorly.

00:11:33.939 --> 00:11:38.569
But who knows, perhaps one day Anderson will
make a triumphant return to Event Horizon

00:11:38.569 --> 00:11:41.579
and blow everyone away with a director's cut.

00:11:41.579 --> 00:11:44.220
"Here I come, motherf----ers!

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.309
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:46.309 --> 00:11:50.119
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
cult classics are coming soon.

00:11:50.119 --> 00:11:53.410
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

